WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:03.529
We’re continuing with our special series
about Argentina.

00:00:03.529 --> 00:00:12.700
(How can they know what it is not to be able
to afford a gas cylinder?)

00:00:12.700 --> 00:00:18.890
(“We bought 300 wagons, 0 kilometers.

00:00:18.890 --> 00:00:19.890
Each of these wagons cost 1 million 90 thousand
dollars.)

00:00:19.890 --> 00:00:22.020
( Peronism is the search for equal opportunities,
the search for social justice, the search

00:00:22.020 --> 00:00:30.130
for zero poverty in Argentina)

00:00:30.130 --> 00:00:39.329
( All Argentines want the same thing, even
if we think differently.)

00:00:39.329 --> 00:00:44.219
Folks, Argentina is one of the few countries
in the world that is undergoing chronic inflation,

00:00:44.219 --> 00:00:45.600
inflation they can’t get rid of.

00:00:45.600 --> 00:00:48.489
When you look at the statistics, it leaves
you speechless.

00:00:48.489 --> 00:00:53.460
This country has experienced high inflationary
processes for at least the last five decades.

00:00:53.460 --> 00:00:55.160
Fifty years!

00:00:55.160 --> 00:01:02.620
In fact, since the Central Bank of Argentina
was created in 1935, the accumulated inflation

00:01:02.620 --> 00:01:08.000
amounts to this spectacular figure that you
see on the screen:

00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:10.399
A crazy 256 thousand trillion percent...

00:01:10.399 --> 00:01:11.399
256,710,707,160,756,000%

00:01:11.399 --> 00:01:14.909
This is the result of an average annual inflation
of more than 60%...

00:01:14.909 --> 00:01:16.759
Since 1935!

00:01:16.759 --> 00:01:22.859
But… why can’t Argentines get rid of inflation?

00:01:22.859 --> 00:01:26.560
What role does the political class, businessmen
and citizens play in all this?

00:01:26.560 --> 00:01:31.099
Why is the dollar so present in the minds
of Argentines?

00:01:31.099 --> 00:01:33.979
Listen up.

00:01:33.979 --> 00:01:47.489
Dear friends, first of all, we need to know
exactly what we mean by inflation.

00:01:47.489 --> 00:01:54.229
It’s often said that there’s inflation
when the prices of the goods and services

00:01:54.229 --> 00:01:58.079
demanded by a country’s population increase
in a general manner.

00:01:58.079 --> 00:01:59.079
But...

00:01:59.079 --> 00:02:00.079
Why does it happen?

00:02:00.079 --> 00:02:01.189
What causes this phenomenon?

00:02:01.189 --> 00:02:08.700
Folks, here’s when that special tool that
we all use to make exchanges comes into play:

00:02:08.700 --> 00:02:10.349
money.

00:02:10.349 --> 00:02:12.010
Well...

00:02:12.010 --> 00:02:17.150
Everyone knows that money, currency, was historically
metallic and its value was given, precisely,

00:02:17.150 --> 00:02:20.590
by each metal’s weight and composition:
Copper, silver, gold...

00:02:20.590 --> 00:02:23.239
But... obviously, this wasn’t very practical.

00:02:23.239 --> 00:02:27.530
So over time, notes that could be converted
into gold and silver began to be issued, as

00:02:27.530 --> 00:02:32.489
they were easier to carry, divide and therefore
use.

00:02:32.489 --> 00:02:38.450
The convertibility system was implemented
until the 30s, when due to the Great Depression,

00:02:38.450 --> 00:02:41.269
countries gradually began to abandon the convertibility
system.

00:02:41.269 --> 00:02:52.709
Finally, in 1971 Richard Nixon gave it the
final punch.

00:02:52.709 --> 00:02:56.200
From then on, folks, the value of bills became
completely dependent on the trust that the

00:02:56.200 --> 00:03:00.430
market had on the economic power, the solvency,
of the country that issued them.

00:03:00.430 --> 00:03:04.340
That’s why the notes issued by the richest
countries usually lead to the greatest value

00:03:04.340 --> 00:03:08.580
and confidence in the market.

00:03:08.580 --> 00:03:14.959
Meanwhile, governments no longer had to stockpile
gold and silver to support their currency.

00:03:14.959 --> 00:03:18.270
The end of convertibility gave the governments
the chance to use that old friend of ours

00:03:18.270 --> 00:03:20.240
as much as they wanted...

00:03:20.240 --> 00:03:22.000
The bill-printing machine.

00:03:22.000 --> 00:03:27.480
Up until then, if a government wanted to spend
more, they either raised taxes or borrowed

00:03:27.480 --> 00:03:28.480
money.

00:03:28.480 --> 00:03:31.659
However, the end of convertibility gave them
another possibility: finance public spending

00:03:31.659 --> 00:03:33.489
with the creation of new money.

00:03:33.489 --> 00:03:37.370
That is, what is popularly known as running
the bill-printing machine.

00:03:37.370 --> 00:03:42.299
Although the truth is that nowadays, you only
need to push a key and… boom, you create

00:03:42.299 --> 00:03:45.680
all the electronic money you want.

00:03:45.680 --> 00:03:47.170
Of course, this has consequences.

00:03:47.170 --> 00:03:51.579
If central banks are prudent and economic
growth accompanies the issuance of new money,

00:03:51.579 --> 00:03:54.370
the effect of inflation won’t be excessively
damaging.

00:03:54.370 --> 00:03:59.079
But when governments pass that barrier...
that’s when a very, very serious problem

00:03:59.079 --> 00:04:00.079
begins.

00:04:00.079 --> 00:04:05.420
Suddenly, folks, an artificial demand is created,
that pushes up the price of goods on the one

00:04:05.420 --> 00:04:08.739
hand, and on the other makes people lose confidence
in that currency...

00:04:08.739 --> 00:04:09.739
The result?

00:04:09.739 --> 00:04:10.739
A bolting inflation.

00:04:10.739 --> 00:04:14.300
Of course, the government responsible for
this situation never wants to acknowledge

00:04:14.300 --> 00:04:19.759
its responsibility, which is why in the end
it always ends up blaming entrepreneurs, accusing

00:04:19.759 --> 00:04:21.880
them of being speculators and greedy.

00:04:21.880 --> 00:04:22.880
Does this sound familiar?

00:04:22.880 --> 00:04:28.410
Then, comes the magic solution – Which only
makes things worse: Price controls.

00:04:28.410 --> 00:04:30.840
Argentina unveils price controls to tame inflation.

00:04:30.840 --> 00:04:31.840
FT)

00:04:31.840 --> 00:04:34.000
And no, don’t think this is a new idea.

00:04:34.000 --> 00:04:35.110
Not at all:

00:04:35.110 --> 00:04:40.380
(Price control in Argentina, a history repeated
by different governments, from Peron to Alfonsin,

00:04:40.380 --> 00:04:43.110
everyone tried to do the same, and were never
successful.

00:04:43.110 --> 00:04:45.360
La Nación.)

00:04:45.360 --> 00:05:00.270
( And it was all destined to end with a scourge
that condemns us all equally...

00:05:00.270 --> 00:05:01.270
That of inflation.

00:05:01.270 --> 00:05:02.270
And we haven’t done anything.)

00:05:02.270 --> 00:05:05.860
Hammurabi's own code (unearthed in 1902),
which was enacted before 2,000 BC, set prices,

00:05:05.860 --> 00:05:10.979
wages, interest rates, and rates, which means
that price controls are at least four thousand

00:05:10.979 --> 00:05:11.979
years old.

00:05:11.979 --> 00:05:14.560
Henry Hazlitt)

00:05:14.560 --> 00:05:18.610
Of course... as this doesn’t work either,
and prices go up even more, politicians usually

00:05:18.610 --> 00:05:23.800
start raising salaries through decrees and
spending more on every kind of plan.

00:05:23.800 --> 00:05:26.949
And all this is financed with even more un-backed
money issuance.

00:05:26.949 --> 00:05:31.270
It’s a vicious circle, that only leads to
more and more inflation, which means destroying

00:05:31.270 --> 00:05:33.850
the value of savings, destroying a country’s
capital;

00:05:33.850 --> 00:05:36.391
annihilating legal security;
preventing business calculation – which

00:05:36.391 --> 00:05:41.759
is essential for making decisions -;
and depreciating the national currency.

00:05:41.759 --> 00:05:48.660
Folks, few countries in the world know this
process better than Argentina.

00:05:48.660 --> 00:05:54.349
(THE LAND OF INFLATION)

00:05:54.349 --> 00:05:59.520
We’ve told you in several videos, Argentina’s
economic situation is terrifying:

00:05:59.520 --> 00:06:04.840
( We’re here today, not because we’re
against anyone.

00:06:04.840 --> 00:06:12.039
We aren’t anti-anyone, we’re in favor
of our future because we think we deserve

00:06:12.039 --> 00:06:13.039
a better life.)

00:06:13.039 --> 00:06:16.729
Little work, low wages, high taxes, disastrous
public accounts and, above all, a lot of inflation.

00:06:16.729 --> 00:06:20.560
Folks, Argentina’s high inflationary process
has been growing steadily since the 1950s.

00:06:20.560 --> 00:06:27.050
(Argentina on the podium: a year-after-year
inflation of 51.3% is the third highest in

00:06:27.050 --> 00:06:30.280
the world only behind Venezuela and Zimbabwe.

00:06:30.280 --> 00:06:31.949
BAE Businesses)

00:06:31.949 --> 00:06:34.240
Now, can you see the process we just described?

00:06:34.240 --> 00:06:37.250
Who on earth is responsible for this inflationary
spiral?

00:06:37.250 --> 00:06:40.940
Well, folks, check out this chart prepared
by the economist Nicolás Cachanosky where

00:06:40.940 --> 00:06:47.970
we can see the lack of control that Argentina's
public accounts are experiencing.

00:06:47.970 --> 00:06:51.840
As you can see, it doesn’t matter who governs,
military, civil, Peronist or radical, the

00:06:51.840 --> 00:06:53.479
result is the same.

00:06:53.479 --> 00:06:58.680
Every Argentine government has devoted itself
to spending uncontrollably, resorting to both

00:06:58.680 --> 00:07:03.560
issuing debt – first internal and then also
external – as well as issuing money.

00:07:03.560 --> 00:07:13.229
And that’s precisely what has caused Argentina’s
current economic disaster.

00:07:13.229 --> 00:07:24.340
Yes, you heard that correctly: Not imports,
not vulture capitalism, not greedy businessmen.

00:07:24.340 --> 00:07:29.810
The truth is that these huge public deficits
have been financed and continue to be financed

00:07:29.810 --> 00:07:35.770
with a huge external debt and very high inflation
rates, that is, with the bill-printing machine.

00:07:35.770 --> 00:07:39.849
Check out this table where you can see the
average annual inflation rates that each Argentinian

00:07:39.849 --> 00:07:49.520
president has had since 1943.

00:07:49.520 --> 00:07:52.250
Some presidents weren’t in power for long,
even less than a year.

00:07:52.250 --> 00:07:54.509
Others were in the presidential chair much
longer.

00:07:54.509 --> 00:07:58.759
Presidents such as the very well-known Perón,
Videla or Alfonsín, Cristina Fernández de

00:07:58.759 --> 00:08:02.969
Kirchner or Mauricio Macri... were very different,
but practically every president had something

00:08:02.969 --> 00:08:05.080
in common: very high inflation rates.

00:08:05.080 --> 00:08:09.030
This process, folks, has been so constant
that Argentines are now used to living with

00:08:09.030 --> 00:08:10.139
huge inflation.

00:08:10.139 --> 00:08:15.180
Of course, that doesn’t lessen their disastrous
economic effects at all, it can even make

00:08:15.180 --> 00:08:16.680
them worse.

00:08:16.680 --> 00:08:21.289
It’s logical that when you get used a quickly
devaluing currency… all market participants

00:08:21.289 --> 00:08:22.289
act accordingly:

00:08:22.289 --> 00:08:26.509
Entrepreneurs try to compensate for risk,
for example by raising prices even more.

00:08:26.509 --> 00:08:30.400
The unions demand wage increases, just in
case, and the government, in an attempt to

00:08:30.400 --> 00:08:34.320
compensate for all the economic and social
problems that have emerged, keep approving

00:08:34.320 --> 00:08:38.280
all kinds of regulations, issuing debt in
foreign currency – because, of course, the

00:08:38.280 --> 00:08:42.100
time comes when local financial markets no
longer serve any purpose – and continue

00:08:42.100 --> 00:08:43.230
to run the bill-printing machine.

00:08:43.230 --> 00:08:45.770
And the problem just accumulates and continues.

00:08:45.770 --> 00:08:51.260
A kind of race organized and driven by politicians
in which all participants lose, especially

00:08:51.260 --> 00:08:54.760
the poorest, those who depend on their salary
to survive.

00:08:54.760 --> 00:08:56.760
Pre-bendarial businessmen, mafia unionists
and ignorant and corrupt politicians.)

00:08:56.760 --> 00:08:58.020
And we can’t fool ourselves.

00:08:58.020 --> 00:09:02.830
A clear result of high inflation is that it
destroys a country’s competitiveness, how?

00:09:02.830 --> 00:09:06.150
Well first by consuming its savings, the local
capital.

00:09:06.150 --> 00:09:10.250
And then, when governments introduce restrictions
of all kinds to deal with the uncontrolled

00:09:10.250 --> 00:09:15.380
price increases, that ends up creating unstable
industries and economies that can’t compete

00:09:15.380 --> 00:09:16.660
in the world.

00:09:16.660 --> 00:09:22.580
This, folks, is precisely what is happening
in Argentina, and that's why Argentina can’t

00:09:22.580 --> 00:09:24.760
get out of a crisis without getting into another.

00:09:24.760 --> 00:09:28.870
Because we’re talking about more than 50
years of mistakes.

00:09:28.870 --> 00:09:32.450
Because Argentinian politicians don’t learn.

00:09:32.450 --> 00:09:38.560
(Recurrent fiscal deficit: (in 2018) the State
covered only 85% of its expenses with actual

00:09:38.560 --> 00:09:39.560
income.

00:09:39.560 --> 00:09:40.560
Infobae.

00:09:40.560 --> 00:09:45.960
Argentina: The debt is now close to 97.7%
of the country's GDP and is the highest in

00:09:45.960 --> 00:09:46.960
the region.

00:09:46.960 --> 00:09:47.960
El Comercio)

00:09:47.960 --> 00:09:49.760
Now, the question is, do Argentinians have
any defense mechanism?

00:09:49.760 --> 00:09:54.150
Is there an instrument that can help them
protect themselves from the stubbornness of

00:09:54.150 --> 00:09:55.150
politicians?

00:09:55.150 --> 00:09:57.340
Yes, in a certain way, there is one.

00:09:57.340 --> 00:10:01.300
Listen up.

00:10:01.300 --> 00:10:06.490
(THE DOLLAR WORLD)

00:10:06.490 --> 00:10:12.960
You don’t need to be an economist to know
that having savings in Argentine pesos is

00:10:12.960 --> 00:10:14.940
a very, very bad idea.

00:10:14.940 --> 00:10:20.460
It’s something that those of you who follow
us from Argentina know all too well.

00:10:20.460 --> 00:10:25.200
So... if your currency is losing value by
the day... in such an significant way, then

00:10:25.200 --> 00:10:29.650
you have no choice but to look for another
currency to better preserve the value of your

00:10:29.650 --> 00:10:30.650
savings.

00:10:30.650 --> 00:10:34.490
And in Argentina’s case, and for virtually
every country in the world with the same problem,

00:10:34.490 --> 00:10:36.490
that currency is the US dollar.

00:10:36.490 --> 00:10:41.500
("The Argentine economy is de facto dollarized,
it must be done for real”.

00:10:41.500 --> 00:10:45.890
Roberto Cachanosky)

00:10:45.890 --> 00:10:49.340
In Argentina, it’s usual for everyone to
know the exchange rate of the dollar to the

00:10:49.340 --> 00:10:50.720
Argentine peso.

00:10:50.720 --> 00:10:57.331
And for Argentinians, the dollar is important
for several reasons: first, because the price

00:10:57.331 --> 00:11:00.360
of products is closely linked to the price
of the dollar.

00:11:00.360 --> 00:11:01.520
Allow me to explain.

00:11:01.520 --> 00:11:06.120
Argentina imports several goods and with their
own currency, the peso, losing value so quickly,

00:11:06.120 --> 00:11:10.410
practically everything is traded in dollars
– which is also the international reserve

00:11:10.410 --> 00:11:11.410
currency.

00:11:11.410 --> 00:11:15.360
This also happens with Argentine exports...
the dollar is more stable and ultimately becomes

00:11:15.360 --> 00:11:16.680
the reference unit.

00:11:16.680 --> 00:11:20.890
Prices in pesos are like a crazy thermometer
that changes temperature constantly.

00:11:20.890 --> 00:11:26.820
Secondly, and most importantly, the dollar
is a currency that allows Argentinians to

00:11:26.820 --> 00:11:28.530
maintain the value of their savings.

00:11:28.530 --> 00:11:33.410
Yes, it’s true that the dollar also suffers
from inflation, but in proportions that can’t

00:11:33.410 --> 00:11:35.800
be compared to the Argentine peso.

00:11:35.800 --> 00:11:40.890
For example, if in 2001 we had saved 20,000
pesos in a drawer, enough at the time for

00:11:40.890 --> 00:11:45.750
an average car, by 2019, with that same money,
we could hardly afford a smartphone.

00:11:45.750 --> 00:11:47.250
And no, it wouldn’t be an iPhone.

00:11:47.250 --> 00:11:55.310
The loss of value of the Argentine Peso is
simply spectacular:

00:11:55.310 --> 00:12:03.730
That, folks, explains why in countries with
high inflation, the dollar circulates almost

00:12:03.730 --> 00:12:05.470
as if it were a national currency.

00:12:05.470 --> 00:12:09.190
It happens in Argentina, and even North Korea
uses dollars.

00:12:09.190 --> 00:12:15.330
Since 1950, Argentina has barely managed to
go 10 years, between 1993 and 2002, with inflation

00:12:15.330 --> 00:12:17.330
at moderate levels, below 4%.

00:12:17.330 --> 00:12:23.360
That’s only ten years in almost seven decades.

00:12:23.360 --> 00:12:30.200
Thus, Argentina has become one of the few
countries in the world that cannot control

00:12:30.200 --> 00:12:31.200
inflation.

00:12:31.200 --> 00:12:34.740
And, folks, make no mistake, the solution
is clear: reduce public spending, balance

00:12:34.740 --> 00:12:38.750
budgets and stop being so dependent on the
central bank.

00:12:38.750 --> 00:12:42.870
Politicians know this, the question is, why
on earth don’t they implement it?

00:12:42.870 --> 00:12:47.260
And this leads me to another question: Will
Argentinian politicians be able to end this

00:12:47.260 --> 00:12:49.700
eternal spiral of inflation?

